course: books
lesson: 1
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter I, part 1: Down the Rabbit-Hole (the fall)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Alice is sitting on a grassy bank next to her big sister on a hot, sleepy day, bored stiff, because her sister's book has no pictures and no talking in it.
explain: She is lazily wondering whether to make a daisy-chain when a White Rabbit with pink eyes runs past, says "I shall be late!", and pulls a watch out of his waistcoat pocket.
explain: Burning with curiosity, Alice dashes after him and jumps down a rabbit-hole without once thinking how she will get out again.
explain: The hole turns into a very deep well, and she falls so slowly that she can look at shelves and cupboards, grab a jar, chatter about the centre of the Earth and the people on the other side of the world, and nearly fall asleep thinking about her cat Dinah.
explain: Then, thump! thump! she lands on a heap of sticks and dry leaves, and the fall is over: a lazy beginning that suddenly turns into a wild, dreamy adventure.
fact: Alice thinks: "and what is the use of a book," "without pictures or conversations?"; "conversations" here means characters talking to each other.
fact: The hot day made her feel "very sleepy and stupid", so she could hardly make up her mind about making a daisy-chain.
fact: The White Rabbit has pink eyes and says "Oh dear! Oh dear! I shall be late!"
fact: Alice did not find a talking rabbit strange at the time; what made her start to her feet was the Rabbit taking "a watch out of its waistcoat-pocket"; a waistcoat is a sleeveless jacket worn over a shirt.
fact: Only later "it occurred to her that she ought to have wondered at this"; at the time a talking rabbit seemed quite natural.
fact: She followed it into a large rabbit-hole under the hedge, "never once considering how in the world she was to get out again".
fact: The hole went straight like a tunnel and then dipped suddenly down into a very deep well.
fact: The sides of the well were filled with cupboards and book-shelves, with maps and pictures hung on pegs.
fact: Alice took a jar labelled "ORANGE MARMALADE" from a shelf; it was empty, and she put it into a cupboard because she did not want to kill somebody underneath by dropping it.
fact: She joked that after such a fall she would think nothing of tumbling downstairs, and would not say anything about it "even if I fell off the top of the house" (which was true, because such a fall would hurt her badly).
fact: Alice guessed she was near the centre of the earth, "four thousand miles down", and said "Latitude" and "Longitude" without knowing what they meant, because they were "nice grand words to say".
fact: She wondered if she would fall right through the earth and come out among people walking upside down and called them "the Antipathies"; she meant the Antipodes, the people on the opposite side of the world, while an antipathy is a strong dislike.
fact: She planned to ask a lady "Please, Ma'am, is this New Zealand or Australia?" but decided not to ask, because the lady would think her "an ignorant little girl"; she tried to curtsey while falling; "ma'am" is a polite word for a lady and a curtsey is a small bow girls used to make.
fact: Dinah is Alice's cat; Alice hoped someone would remember Dinah's saucer of milk at tea-time, and said there were no mice in the air, but Dinah might catch a bat, which is "very like a mouse".
fact: Sleepy, Alice kept asking "Do cats eat bats?" and sometimes "Do bats eat cats?", because she could not answer either question, so the order did not matter; then she began to doze and dreamed she was walking hand in hand with Dinah.
fact: Twice in this part a paragraph begins "Down, down, down." to show how long the fall goes on.
fact: She landed on "a heap of sticks and dry leaves" and "was not a bit hurt".
fact: Old words in today's English: "presently" = soon after, "fancy" = just imagine, "to-night" = tonight, "I should think" = I expect.
exercise: Where was Alice sitting at the very start of the book? | On the bank, next to her sister | In a classroom with her teacher | Under the hedge by the rabbit-hole | Beside the fire, with her cat Dinah
exercise: Why did Alice think her sister's book was no use? | It had no pictures or conversations | It was much too long and heavy to read | It was written in French and Latin | It was all about rabbits and their holes
exercise: What was Alice wondering about just before the Rabbit came? | Whether a daisy-chain was worth the trouble | Whether to go home for a nice cup of tea | Whether her sister would read to her aloud | Whether to have a swim in the river
exercise: What colour were the White Rabbit's eyes? | Pink | Blue | Black | Green
exercise: What did the Rabbit say to itself as it ran past? | "Oh dear! Oh dear! I shall be late!" | "Hurry up, little girl, and follow me down!" | "Where are my gloves and my fan?" | "Good afternoon, young lady!"
exercise: What finally made Alice start to her feet? | The Rabbit took a watch out of its waistcoat-pocket | The Rabbit spoke to itself in plain English | The Rabbit had bright pink eyes and white fur | The Rabbit ran so very close to her sister
exercise: What is a waistcoat? | A sleeveless jacket worn over a shirt | A long coat that reaches to the ankles | A warm scarf tied around the waist | A belt with a small pocket for coins
exercise: What did Alice NOT think about before jumping into the hole? | How she would ever get out again | Whether the Rabbit was going to be late | What the Rabbit's watch might look like | Whether the Rabbit was going home
exercise: What lined the sides of the deep well? | Cupboards, book-shelves, maps and pictures | Roots, stones, worms and the warm nests of moles | Doors, keys and little glass tables | Lamps, mirrors and spiders' webs
exercise: What was written on the jar she took from a shelf? | "ORANGE MARMALADE" | "DRINK ME" | "STRAWBERRY JAM" | "POISON"
exercise: What was inside the marmalade jar? | Nothing; it was empty | Thick orange marmalade | A tiny golden key | A note from the Rabbit
exercise: Why didn't Alice just let go of the empty jar? | She feared it might kill somebody underneath | She meant to fill it with jam back at home | She thought it belonged to the White Rabbit | She wanted to keep it as a souvenir of the trip
exercise: How deep did Alice think the centre of the earth was? | About four thousand miles down | About forty miles down | About four hundred and forty miles down | About four million miles down
exercise: Why did Alice say "Latitude" and "Longitude"? | They were nice grand words to say | She knew exactly where she was on the map | Her sister had asked her to practise them | They were written on the walls of the well
exercise: What did Alice call the people on the other side of the world? | The Antipathies | The Antipodes | The Australians | The Upside-downs
exercise: Which word did she really mean? | The Antipodes | The Antarctic | The Antelopes | The Atlantic
exercise: What does "antipathy" really mean? | A strong dislike | A faraway country | An upside-down person | A kind of map
exercise: Which two countries did Alice think she might come out in? | New Zealand or Australia | France or Germany | America or Canada or Mexico | India or China
exercise: What did Alice try to do while falling, as if meeting a lady? | Curtsey | Shake hands | Sing a song | Wave a handkerchief
exercise: Who is Dinah? | Alice's cat | Alice's sister | The Rabbit's maid | A lady in Australia
exercise: What did Alice hope people at home would remember for Dinah? | Her saucer of milk at tea-time | To brush her fur before bed | To let her out into the garden | To give her a mouse to play with
exercise: What did Alice say Dinah might catch in the air instead of mice? | A bat | A bird | A fly | A moth
exercise: What did Alice land on at the bottom? | A heap of sticks and dry leaves | A soft feather mattress | The cold, hard stone floor of a long hall | A pool of salty water
exercise: How was Alice after landing? | Not a bit hurt | Badly bruised all over | Too dizzy to stand up | Crying with a sore knee
exercise: Here "presently" means... | Soon after | Right now | As a gift | Never again
test: Mia goes: "Alice saw a talking rabbit and was like, whoa, that's mad, and ran after it." Put her right. | The talking seemed natural; the watch amazed her | She ran because the Rabbit called her name | She ran because her sister told her to follow | She never ran; the Rabbit pulled her down
test: Tayo, Gen Alpha: "Alice jumped in the hole with zero plan, that's so delulu." ("delulu" = living in a dream, not facing facts.) Which line from the book backs him up? | "never once considering how in the world she was to get out again" | "the wise little Alice was not going to do that in a hurry" | "she had read several nice little histories about children" | "it occurred to her that she ought to have wondered at this"
test: Why does the book say Alice's joke about falling off the top of the house was "very likely true"? | After such a fall she would be too hurt to say anything | Her family never listened to a word she said | Her house had no stairs and no roof to fall from at all | She was always very brave about little bumps
test: What would have happened if Alice had dropped the jar? | It might have hit and hurt somebody underneath | It would have floated back up to the shelf | It would have turned into a little cake | Nothing; it was made of soft paper
test: Nan, a bit cross: "Fancy curtseying while you're falling! Ridiculous." Why is it funny? | You cannot bow politely while tumbling through the air | Curtseying was only allowed in church long ago | Alice did not know how to curtsey at all | Nobody was there, so curtseying was rude
test: Kieran from Dublin: "Ah she's an eejit, she thought she'd land in Australia, so she did." ("eejit" = idiot.) What did Alice actually wonder? | Whether she would fall right through the earth | Whether the Rabbit was going to Australia | Whether her sister had moved to New Zealand | Whether the well had a map of the world
test: Why did Alice decide not to ask the lady which country it was? | She feared the lady would think her an ignorant little girl | She could not speak the lady's language at all | She was falling in far too much of a hurry to stop and have a chat | The lady would be upside down and unable to hear
test: True or false: Alice knew exactly what Latitude and Longitude meant. | False: she had no idea, but liked the grand words | True: she had learnt them that very morning | True: she used them to find out where she was | False: she had never heard the words before
test: "Do cats eat bats? Do bats eat cats?" Why did the order of the words not matter to Alice? | She could not answer either question | Cats and bats are the very same animal | She was talking to Dinah, who could answer | Bats really do eat cats now and then
test: Callum, rough: "Shut up about your stupid cat, we're falling down a hole here!" Who in the story talks about Dinah while falling? | Alice, to herself | The White Rabbit | Alice's sister | The lady in Australia
test: Gran from Glasgow: "Wee Alice was dreamin' the whole way doon, aye?" ("wee" = little, "doon" = down.) Which is true? | She began to doze and dream about Dinah | She slept soundly from top to bottom | She stayed wide awake and counted shelves | She dreamed about the White Rabbit's watch
test: What would a real person probably do if they fell four thousand miles? | Get badly hurt, so the gentle fall shows it is dreamlike | Land softly on leaves just like Alice did | Arrive safely in Australia after about an hour of falling | Bounce back up to the top of the hole
test: Why does the narrator say there was "nothing so very remarkable" in seeing the Rabbit at first? | Rabbits are common, and she thought little of it then | Alice had a pet white rabbit at home already | The Rabbit was a toy her sister had brought along | Alice was asleep and did not see it properly
test: ★★★ Read carefully: "it occurred to her that she ought to have wondered at this". When did Alice think the talking rabbit strange? | Afterwards, when she thought it over | At once, the moment it spoke | Only when it took out its watch | Never, not even afterwards
test: ★★★ Alice says the centre of the earth is "four thousand miles down". If she has fallen all the way to the middle, how much farther to come out on the other side? | About four thousand miles more | About two thousand miles more | No farther; the middle is the other side | About eight thousand miles more
test: ★★★ Alice says "Down, down, down" and the narrator says it twice. How many separate times does the phrase "Down, down, down" begin a paragraph in this part? | Two | One | Three | Four
exam: Identify the occurrence which induced the protagonist to rise to her feet and pursue the White Rabbit. | The removal by the Rabbit of a watch from its waistcoat-pocket | The Rabbit's audible declaration that it should be late for an appointment | The observation that the Rabbit possessed pink eyes | An instruction issued by the protagonist's sister
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the protagonist declined to release the jar during her descent. | Apprehension that it might cause the death of a person below | A wish to retain the marmalade for subsequent consumption | The belief that it was the property of the White Rabbit | Insufficient strength to open her hand while falling
exam: It is given that the protagonist intended to refer to the Antipodes. Determine the term she in fact employed. | The Antipathies | The Antarctics | The Antiquities | The Anticipations
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the protagonist's condition upon reaching the bottom. | She sustained no injury whatsoever | She sustained a fracture of the ankle | She was rendered wholly unconscious | She was immersed in salt water
